{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793889563,"nonce":"Lb2FB41Ji23bjJE+WxhW2cx7Ydk=","title":"Avoid operand-vector allocations in scalar replacement","message":"Follow-up to 1be184c and b9837845fd7abe23b2cd8bc8a24a80fca1339cc8. scalar_replace_alloc calls HirInstruction::operands for its cross-block use index and local escape scans, allocating a temporary Vec per instruction. HIR already provides for_each_operand and any_operand, and operands itself delegates to the visitor.\n\nA baseline allocation-audit fixture scalarizing one malloc records 29 allocations / 2644 bytes for 1 arithmetic instruction, versus 2035 allocations / 313172 bytes for 1000. The new bounded-request regression fails on the baseline as expected.\n\nUse the existing visitors, including the terminator visitor, preserving indirect callees, phi edges and encoded-pointer uses. Validate the allocation scaling, an indirect-callee-only escape, existing scalarization behavior, full official TS corpus and Lua/Python conformance. Measure frozen binaries on an idle NUC with LLVM off and snapshots enabled.","files":null}]}